---
title: WebSocket conversations
intent: Send, match, extract and close using a local WebSocket server.
group: resilience
features:
- websocket
- chaining
command: curlew run testapi/collections/91-websocket.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
order: 80
runnable: true
---

Place `websocket:` inside `request:`. With `expect.count` greater than one, extraction yields a JSON-encoded array of matched values, not just the last value. The fixture below also checks fragmented messages and connection behavior.

## Prerequisites

Use the complete repository checkout and start Mudflat as described in the [cookbook setup](https://github.com/weiqigod/curlew/blob/main/site/README.md). Run these commands from the repository root with `curlew` on PATH.

```bash
export MUDFLAT_URL="${MUDFLAT_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export MUDFLAT_WS_URL="${MUDFLAT_WS_URL:-ws://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export RUN_ID="cookbook-$(date +%s)-$$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run testapi/collections/91-websocket.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
```

A successful execution exits 0. Read assertion outcomes as well as the exit code.
No example output or timing numbers are fabricated here.

## Complete collection

<!-- cookbook-source: testapi/collections/91-websocket.yaml -->
```yaml
# WebSocket against a server that writes its own frames.
#
# mudflat's WebSocket layer is written from RFC 6455 rather than taken from
# gorilla, which is what curlew's client uses. A mudflat built on gorilla would
# agree with curlew by construction about masking, fragmentation, control frames
# and close codes — every bug in the shared reading invisible, which is the
# closed-loop problem of specification §3 wearing a different hat.
#
# Note where the block goes: `websocket:` is INSIDE `request:`. docs/MANUAL.md
# §7.2 puts it a level higher, and a collection written from that example is
# rejected with "must have websocket.steps with at least one action".
name: WebSocket
description: |
  Send, expect, count, extract, wait and close against nine endpoints, plus
  subprotocol negotiation, a delayed handshake, and message reassembly across
  continuation frames.

requests:
  - name: send and echo, three times over one connection
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/echo"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: send
            message:
              hello: "mudflat"
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 5000
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "echo" }
              $.seq: { equals: 1 }
              $.binary: { equals: false }
          - action: send
            message_raw: "a raw string, not JSON"
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 5000
            message:
              $.seq: { equals: 2 }
              $.text: { equals: "a raw string, not JSON" }
          - action: close
            code: 1000
            reason: "done"

  - name: three unsolicited messages, counted
    description: |
      The server sends without being asked. Sequence numbers are the reason the
      envelope carries one: `count: 3` satisfied by a single message matching
      three times would show three identical numbers here.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/push?n=3&ms=5"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 5000
            count: 3
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "push" }
            extract:
              push_seqs: "$.seq"

  - name: a counted extraction yields a JSON array, not the last value
    description: |
      The undocumented half of `count:`. With count > 1 each extracted name
      becomes a JSON-encoded array of the per-message values — here
      ["1","2","3"] — rather than the value from the final message.

      Neither docs/MANUAL.md nor docs/CLI_SPECIFICATION.md says so, and the
      manual's own example names the variable `last_order_id`, which implies
      exactly the opposite. A collection author following it gets an array
      where they expected an id, and finds out when the next request sends
      `["ord_1","ord_2","ord_3"]` to a URL.

      This request is here to state the real contract in a form that fails if
      it ever changes.
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/echo?seqs={{push_seqs}}"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        # The array reaches the wire verbatim: an interpolated value is not
        # percent-encoded on its way into a query string.
        $.request.target: { contains: '["1","2","3"]' }

  - name: one message split across three continuation frames
    description: |
      Each fragment is invalid JSON on its own, so a client that mistook a
      frame for a message would fail to parse rather than quietly assert
      against a third of the document.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/fragmented"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 5000
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "fragmented" }
              $.text: { equals: "three frames, one message" }

  - name: a subprotocol is negotiated from the offered list
    description: |
      Server preference wins: mudflat.v2 is first server-side and second in the
      client's list. That the header arrives at all is the other half — the
      dialer takes headers from the request, and this is where they either
      survive the upgrade or do not.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/subprotocol"
      headers:
        Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: "mudflat.v1, mudflat.v2"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 5000
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "negotiated" }
              $.subprotocol: { equals: "mudflat.v2" }

  - name: a handshake delayed by 300ms still completes
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/slow-accept?ms=300"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 5000
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "late" }
              $.text: { contains: "300ms" }

  - name: a close code other than 1000 arrives before the close
    description: |
      The close code itself is surfaced — "websocket: close 4000: mudflat
      closing with 4000" — which is the §9.L outcome that came out
      affirmative. It cannot be asserted from a collection, because a step
      that reads past the close is an error rather than an assertion, so the
      pre-close message is what this request pins.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/close/4000"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 5000
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "closing" }
              $.text: { contains: "4000" }

  - name: a heartbeat survives while a step is reading
    description: |
      The A half of an A/B pair. Here an expect step reads continuously, the
      pongs are dispatched, and the heartbeat holds for five intervals. A
      passed even while §11C.5 was open, which is what made the finding
      attributable to dispatch rather than to the server.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/push?n=5&ms=150"
      websocket:
        heartbeat:
          enabled: true
          interval_ms: 100
        steps:
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 8000
            count: 5
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "push" }

  - name: a heartbeat survives while a step is idle
    description: |
      The B half, and the one that matters: a heartbeat exists to hold an IDLE
      connection open. This was §11C.5, and it lived in
      testapi/gaps/expected-failures.yaml because it failed at the first
      interval —

        step 1 (wait): websocket heartbeat timeout after 100ms

      — against /ws/echo, which answers every ping (pinned server-side by
      TestWS_EchoAnswersAClientPing, without which this would not be
      attributable). The feature was inverted: it failed precisely when idle,
      and reported a server that answered correctly as dead.

      gorilla dispatches control frames only from inside ReadMessage, and a
      `wait` step slept on a timer. The pong landed on the socket and the
      handler never ran. Reads now go through a single pump that stays inside
      ReadMessage for the life of the connection, so pongs are dispatched
      whether or not a step is reading.

      Four intervals of idling, then a round trip to prove the connection is
      still usable afterwards — a heartbeat that survived by killing the
      connection would not be a fix.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/echo"
      websocket:
        heartbeat:
          enabled: true
          interval_ms: 100
        steps:
          - action: wait
            duration_ms: 400
          - action: send
            message: { after: "an idle period" }
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 3000
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "echo" }

  - name: a message arriving during a wait is not lost
    description: |
      A wait reads now rather than sleeping, so it necessarily consumes frames
      that arrive while it runs. They go to the same buffer an expect step
      searches first, which is what keeps the change from silently dropping
      messages.

      /ws/push sends three messages 50ms apart. The wait covers all of them,
      and the expect that follows must still find all three — from the buffer,
      with nothing left on the wire.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/push?n=3&ms=50"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: wait
            duration_ms: 400
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 1000
            count: 3
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "push" }

  - name: curlew answers a protocol ping
    description: |
      The client half of the heartbeat contract, and the only place it is
      observable. mudflat sends two protocol pings, waits for the pongs, and
      reports the count in a data message — a collection can assert on a
      message and cannot assert on a close reason.

      pongs: 2 means curlew replied to both. A client that never pongs would
      report 0 here without anything else in the run changing, which is what
      makes the /ws/no-pong side of the pair attributable.
    request:
      protocol: websocket
      url: "{{ws}}/ws/ping?n=2&ms=50"
      websocket:
        steps:
          - action: expect
            timeout_ms: 8000
            message:
              $.type: { equals: "ping-report" }
              $.pings: { equals: 2 }
              $.pongs: { equals: 2 }

  - name: an upgrade rejected for an unsupported subprotocol
    description: |
      The negative control for negotiation. The dial fails, so this asserts on
      the HTTP response rather than on a WebSocket: mudflat answers 400 with a
      body naming what it does speak.
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/ws/subprotocol"
      headers:
        Connection: "Upgrade"
        Upgrade: "websocket"
        Sec-WebSocket-Version: "13"
        Sec-WebSocket-Key: "dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ=="
        Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: "something.else"
    assertions:
      status: 400
      body:
        $.detail: { contains: "mudflat.v2" }

  - name: a request that is not an upgrade at all
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/ws/echo"
    assertions:
      status: 400
      body:
        $.detail: { contains: "RFC 6455" }
```
